  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nAt the time this discussion was published, an MCV was tracking \neast over US-24. Meanwhile north of the MCV, widespread rain is \nfalling with isolated to widely scattered lightning. This system\nwill continue to track east through the afternoon.\n\nThere could be a break in the late afternoon for areas south of\nInterstate 80, as continued rain is expected north along a\nprojected low level vorticity axis and 500 mb jet axis along the\nIllinois/Wisconsin state line. With the current MCV moving\nthrough the area, it is difficult to pinpoint the exact location\nof the stationary front that was previously analyzed just south\nof the forecast area. That becomes important because the right \nentrance region of the previously mentioned 500 mb jet (and\ntherefore area of focused upper level divergence) will be\nsituated just north of the stationary front. High-res CAMs are\nprojecting weak perturbations within the 500 mb flow this\nevening and through the overnight. With precipitable water\namounts between 1.5 to 2 inches, this set-up is increasing\nconcerns for flooding threat in the area. The one fly in the\nointment for this set-up is the absence of a strong low level\njet. Had there been a stronger low level jet projected, perhaps\nthere would need to be consideration to expand the previously\nissued Flood Watch northward. Showers are expected to develop\nover northern Illinois with the risk of training storms over the\nsame area. It is no surprise that ensembles are showing a broad\nswath of 1 to 2 inches of rain that could fall overnight, with \nan axis of locally higher amounts over the southwest portions of\nthe forecast area.\n\nThere is lower confidence in the exact timing of when the rain\nends on Friday morning. As the upper level jet weakens and moves\neast, rain should slowly taper off by mid-morning with the\npotential for a drier period expected in the afternoon. However,\nas the broad upper level ridge over the southeastern portion of\nthe CONUS remains in place, another short wave will ride over\nthe top of the ridge tomorrow afternoon and evening providing\nenough forcing for the next system to develop in Iowa and move\neast. Rain could begin in the early evening, but the better\nchances for the heavier downpours are expected closer to\nmidnight when the core of the wave moves over. Given what the\nforecast is for rainfall overnight tonight on already saturated\nsoils, there is growing concerns for flooding threats\ntomorrow night into Saturday morning and collaborated with the \nWeather Prediction Center to increase the risk to a level 3 out\nof 4 for excessive rainfall. The potential for water loaded\ndownbursts keeps much of the forecast area in a marginal (level\n1 out 5) risk for severe storms tomorrow night, but weaker shear\nprofiles keep the confidence in severe development low.\n\nModels have shown fairly good agreement that yet another wave is\nexpected to pass over the ridge on Saturday providing another\nthreat for heavy rainfall and flooding threat across portions of\nthe forecast area. Global models are not suggesting a break in\nthe rain until a front drops down out of Wisconsin late on\nSunday. Surface high pressure is expected to grow on Monday for\nthe potential for a dry day in the region. However, global\nmodels are still suggesting another system potentially bringing\na a renewed threat for precipitation mid-week next week.\n\nDK",
